  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It builds a 15-box pyramid (5/4/3/2/1) on a static floor and a heavy ball, all as Rapier rigid bodies bound to scene nodes. The code typechecks. I couldn't run it in a browser here, so I haven't seen the scene, the shadows, the camera framing or the crash.\n\n- **Launch:** the ball starts on the floor to the left of the pyramid. About 1.5 s after load (simulated time) the code sets its velocity to [22, 3.5, 0], so it flies toward the pyramid in a slight arc.\n- **Physics stepping:** physics advances in fixed 1/60 s steps from `onFrame`, so the boxes tumble and settle after the hit.\n- **Space:** the keydown handler calls `app.setScene(buildScene())`, which restores every box to its start pose and orientation, then relaunches about two physics steps later. I used `setScene` because the body handles can't reset rotation. I didn't confirm that it rebuilds the physics bodies cleanly. A failed launch after a reset is silently retried each frame.\n- **Lighting and camera:** the scene uses a neutral environment plus a shadow-casting directional light. The perspective camera sits in front, framing both the ball's path and the pyramid.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first `requestAnimationFrame` after `app.ready()`.\n\n",
